CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 2 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
`handling_datarace`'s write-side event collection excludes any write whose
containing function name starts with "pthread" (`is_pthread`), so that
`pthread_mutex_lock`/`_unlock`/`_init`'s own internal bookkeeping fields
(owner/count/recursive) are never misreported as user data races. The filter
was too broad: it also excluded `pthread_create`'s write to its own
thread-ID *output parameter* -- memory the caller supplied, not library
state. A thread reading that thread-ID global with no synchronisation at
all was reported race-free.

This program has exactly that race: one thread's `pthread_create` writes the
global `tid`, another thread reads it via `pthread_join(tid, ...)` with no
lock anywhere. Before the fix: SUCCESSFUL (the write was invisible to the
race encoding). After: FAILED.

This is also the root cause behind `per-thread-array-join-counter-race{,-2,
-3,-4}` on the corpus answering wrong/unknown -- `tids[i]` is written only
through `pthread_create`. Fixing this alone does not flip those four: a
second, separate bug (asymmetric read-offset computation when the array
index is read as a function-call argument, e.g. `pthread_join(tids[i], ...)`)
still hides the race there. See project memory for that part.
